{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S6 — Bounded concurrent gate executions, with fast-fail admission\n\nImplements ADR 0112 decision 5; a comment on this ticket carries the fuller reasoning.\n\n## Why this story exists\n\nOperator: **~10 plan reviews run in parallel frequently**, plus code reviews and autodeploy on\nthe same volume. S2–S5 cap each accumulator via a high-water-mark GC, and **a watermark GC\nassumes evictable data exists**. Under ~10 concurrent gate runs it largely does not.\n\n`src/rebar/_snapshot/janitor.py` documents the mechanism: it relies on POSIX\n**delete-on-last-close** plus touch-on-read mtime recency and takes **no per-reader lease** — a\nPID+heartbeat lease was spiked and REJECTED as unsound (N readers per entry, PID reuse,\ncrash-stale leases). So the janitor may `unlink` an in-flight snapshot, but the blocks are not\nreturned until the last reader closes.\n\n**Consequence: at peak concurrency the held bytes are LIVE, not garbage.** A cap below peak\nconcurrent hold cannot be honoured — the GC runs, finds nothing evictable, and the volume fills\nanyway. Same shape as bug 3276, which measured prune's reclaimable ceiling at ~1.06 GB against a\n29 GB problem. Bounded concurrency is a **precondition** for S2–S5, not an optimization.\n\n## Scope\n\nBoth **plan-review** and **completion-verifier** create a copy of the repo at a ref, so both\nconsume the bounded resource and **share ONE counter** — two caps of N each would admit 2N\nholders, which is the bound that was needed.\n\n**At capacity the call FAST-FAILS.** It does not queue and does not block. Queueing is rejected:\na queued gate still holds its thread and its ~739 MB resident while waiting, so a queue converts\ndisk pressure into memory pressure, and it still holds the MCP client's request past its ~60 s\ndeadline. Shedding load is the correct primitive.\n\n## Design\n\n**Seam.** `rebar.llm.peak_rss.gate_peak_rss` already wraps exactly this scope, and its docstring\nstates why: wrapping the library entry points `review_plan()` / `verify_completion()` \"covers\nboth call paths at one seam, since both reach the gate through these functions\". Admission takes\nthe same seam — a sibling context manager `rebar.llm.gate_admission.gate_admission`, entered\nimmediately outside `gate_peak_rss` in both entry points. One wrapper, both gates, both AC4\npaths, one counter. This extends the existing pre-clone admission point\n(`janitor.ensure_min_free_space` / `SnapshotLowDiskError` / `low_disk.py`) with a second term\nrather than building a parallel mechanism.\n\n**Counter.** N slot files under the snapshot store's existing `locks/` directory, each held for\nthe run with a non-blocking `fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)` and released in a `finally`. Cross\n-process (AC4: CLI gates are separate processes) and crash-safe by construction (AC5: the kernel\ndrops a flock when the holder dies or its fd closes — no PID heartbeat, which the janitor already\nrejected). It serialises between threads of one process too, because a flock is held by the open\nfile description and each acquisition opens its own. It inherits `REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR`, so it\nfollows gate scratch onto ADR 0112 decision 3's dedicated volume for free.\n\n**Refusal.** `GateCongestedError` — an exception, never a verdict value, so it cannot be read as\nINDETERMINATE or as a BLOCK (AC3). The message names congestion, the cap, and that the caller\nshould retry. CLI maps it to **exit 11**, already documented in this repo as \"transient — retry\",\nrather than minting a new code. MCP returns a structured `{error, message, retryable: true}`\npayload in the shape `_structured_llm_failure` already produces, so an agent branches on\n`retryable` rather than parsing English. On the review-bot path this is ADR 0069's retryable\ndeferral shape and must never become an `LLM-Review −1`.\n\n**Metric (AC7).** One line-start `GATE_CONGESTED {json}` stderr marker, copying the\n`GATE_PEAK_RSS` / `VOTER_ERROR` / `MERGE_CHANGE_ERROR` convention verbatim. That convention\nexists because the containers hold no AWS credentials and the host probe greps journald; it is\nalso the portable choice (`project.portability`) — identical behaviour under the CLI on a laptop\nwith no CloudWatch and no CI provider, so the capability is not CI-triggered.\n\n**Config (AC6).** `[snapshot].max_concurrent_gates`, resolved by `resolve_gate_max_concurrent`\nbeside `resolve_gate_min_free_bytes`. `0` disables the bound (the off-switch idiom `max_bytes` /\n`max_entries` already use); a malformed value falls back to the default rather than crashing\nevery gate, matching the stated posture of the neighbouring resolver.\n\n**Recorded deviation from ADR 0112 decision 6 — no `REBAR_GATE_*` env tier.** A new\n`REBAR_GATE_MAX_CONCURRENT` is a new `env_var` mechanism, and bug `a1f1-a30d-2d50-4f5a` records\nthat a new mechanism is currently unlandable (a marker satisfies the gate script but\n`tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py` asserts `new == 0` ignoring markers, and every baseline\nroute is closed to a contributor). `[snapshot]` is a `_RESERVED_SECTIONS` member, absent from the\n`_SECTIONS` the config detectors read, so the config tier lands cleanly. The env tier is one line\nonce `a1f1` is fixed.\n\n**Default = 4, derived from measurement.** plan_review `peak_rss_bytes` ~748 MB,\nverify_completion ~462 MB (`GATE_PEAK_RSS`; ADR 0112 cites ~739 MB / ~4.7 min and a degraded run\nat 501 MB, so it varies run to run). The host is a `t4g.large` (8 GiB) shared with Gerrit and\nADR 0112 decision 7 forbids growing it; steady state ~2.17 GB and Gerrit reserves ~3 GiB by\nconfig (`heapLimit 2g` + `packedGitLimit 1g`), leaving roughly 3 GiB for gate work. 4 × 748 MB ≈\n2.99 GB fits; a fifth run (~3.74 GB) does not. 4 is also decisively below the observed ~10\nconcurrent, which is the point — a cap at or above observed peak sheds no load.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: a configurable limit bounds concurrent plan-review AND completion-verifier executions\n  against ONE shared counter, with a default derived from measurement. Verified by a test that\n  holds `limit` slots and asserts the next admission is rejected while they run.\n- [ ] AC2: at capacity the call FAST-FAILS — it does not block, queue, or wait. Verified by a\n  test asserting the rejected call returns within a small bound rather than after a release.\n- [ ] AC3: the rejection is actionable and unambiguous — it names congestion, says to retry, and\n  is distinguishable in type/shape from a gate verdict (not INDETERMINATE, not FAIL). Verified by\n  a test asserting the error shape over the MCP surface and the CLI exit code.\n- [ ] AC4: the bound covers BOTH the in-process MCP daemon path and separate-process CLI runs\n  against the same counter. Verified by a test exercising a real second process.\n- [ ] AC5: releasing is crash-safe — a gate process that dies does not permanently consume a\n  slot. Verified by a test that kills a holder and asserts reclamation, and by a test that a\n  raising gate releases its slot (a leak on the error path degrades to deadlock).\n- [ ] AC6: the limit is a documented config key with a stated default and rationale, not a\n  hardcoded constant. Verified by a test reading the resolver and by `docs/config.md`.\n- [ ] AC7: rejections publish a metric so congestion is visible rather than silent. Verified by a\n  test asserting emission on a rejected admission.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py -q`\n- `python -m pytest tests -k \"gate_admission or low_disk\" -q`\n- `make lint && make typecheck && python -m pytest tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py -q`\n  (the last is not covered by `make lint` and is the gate that catches a new mechanism name)\n\n## File impact\n\n`src/rebar/llm/gate_admission.py` (new), `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/__init__.py`,\n`src/rebar/llm/completion.py`, `src/rebar/_config_resolvers.py`, `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py`,\n`src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py`, `docs/config.md`, `docs/plan-review-gate.md`,\n`tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py` (new)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788491727773503001,"uuid":"41059c28-5518-4929-92d6-449f789282b1"}